> ## Step-by-Step
  
  1. Locate the cost or expressivity bottleneck inside the multi-stage pipeline and pin it to one concrete structural d
egree of freedom — the *placement* of trainable parameters, the *wiring* between stages, or the *primitive operator* th
at aggregates or represents at each stage. Name the exact element you intend to move or swap; pointing only at 'the arc
hitecture is expensive' or 'this module over-generalizes' reads as an undifferentiated tweak and gives reviewers nothin
g to evaluate as a contribution.
  2. Identify a surrogate that can occupy the same structural slot while changing the targeted property — ideally one g
rounded in a classical theorem, a bijectively equivalent representation, or a revived classical construction that the f
ield had dismissed or never connected to this slot. Re-derive why the surrogate fits *this* problem's structure; a surr
ogate lifted wholesale from a separate application domain, with a coupling mechanism bolted on, will be read as borrowi
ng rather than insight unless its fit to the target task is argued from first principles.
  3. Prove or directly measure that the substitution preserves the essential property the architecture relied on — univ
ersal approximation, a required symmetry or constraint, stable gradient flow, or an exact capacity/representational equ
ivalence between old and new slot. The preservation argument must reveal new structure: a proof that is a trivial adapt
ation of an existing result, or that establishes a property orthogonal to the one the swap was meant to buy, leaves the
 load-bearing claim unsupported.
  4. Demonstrate that the surrogate not only matches the original but *unlocks something the original could not* — a st
rictly larger object class, configuration-agnostic generalization, symbolic extractability, emergent compression, or a 
property (parallelism, constant-time inference, manifold faithfulness) that the original slot structurally forbids. Sho
wing mere parity, or a marginal metric gain at added cost, collapses the work into an incremental variant of the archit
ecture it replaces.
  5. Validate the unlocked gain in the exact regime and at the scale where it is claimed, against baselines matched to 
that specific property. Reporting a secondary proxy — throughput without accuracy, toy-scale data, or no matched-compre
ssion/matched-parameter comparison — while leaving the headline property unverified is the single most common reason an
 otherwise-sound substitution is rejected.
  
