+++ b/ideaspark_run/world-action-model-quantization/phase2_generate/phase2_generate_output.json
@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+{
+  "title": "Causal Consequence Calibration for Quantized World Action Models",
+  "hook": "At a matched high-precision-unit budget, the candidate predicts that the rank correlation between local PTQ discrepancy and paired one-step closed-loop consequence will drop specifically on contact-transition states, while consequence-ranked protection will reduce contact-sensitive task failures relative to local-score ranking. The surprising result is the regime split, not a claim that local numerical error is useless everywhere.",
+  "hook_shape_rationale": "(a) Surprising empirical reveal: the named Spearman rank correlation between local discrepancy and the counterfactual consequence index should be lower on simulator-identified contact transitions, and the same fixed-budget allocation should improve the downstream contact-sensitive task metric. Shape (c) was viable because all allocations use the same precision budget, but it was rejected because the method adds calibration rollouts rather than being strictly no-more-cost on a load-bearing resource.",
+  "gap_closure": [
+    {
+      "gap": "additive: No retrieved WAM-quantization leaf establishes the downstream consequence of a numerical perturbation at a module or denoising step for the final action chunk and the next closed-loop observation in contact-critical manipulation; this is the unresolved residue of QuantWAMs (arxiv:2607.28405v1), not a request for a particular remedy. — stakes: practitioners may deploy a memory- and latency-efficient WAM that preserves local calibration metrics but fails on contact-sensitive actions, while researchers may mistake local fidelity for transferable closed-loop reliability.",
+      "main_pattern": "reframe_as_solvable_object",
+      "sub_pattern": "C00 (Reframe as a Solvable Object)",
+      "companion_pattern": "architectural_operator_substitution",
+      "how_closed": "Closed by recasting each numerical intervention as a per-site counterfactual action-and-next-observation consequence functional, then using the companion at the main-pattern level to replace the layer-local fidelity score with a runnable precision-scoring and allocation operator."
+    },
+    {
+      "gap": "subtractive: No retrieved WAM-quantization leaf tests whether the local numerical-fidelity surrogate inherited from layer-local PTQ is actually sufficient for precision selection in the coupled video/action setting, rather than an assumed proxy for downstream task impact; this questions the inherited assumption and leaves the replacement open. — stakes: precision-allocation conclusions can rest on an unexamined surrogate and invalidate comparisons across WAMs or tasks, while deployment teams cannot tell whether an apparent speed or memory gain preserves the action behavior they require.",
+      "main_pattern": "controlled_diagnostic_design",
+      "sub_pattern": "C02 (Design a Confound-Isolating Diagnostic)",
+      "companion_pattern": null,
+      "how_closed": "Closed by evaluating local fidelity and the counterfactual consequence on the same site, history, denoising seed, simulator state, and precision budget, then reporting their divergence separately for contact and non-contact transitions."
+    }
+  ],
+  "core_mechanism": "The shared object is a per-site counterfactual consequence index C_r for a numerical quantization site r, where r is a tuple of WAM branch, module, inner denoising step, and weight-or-activation axis; the site registry is enumerated from the public coupled WAM's actual PTQ hooks, so no latent rollout/planning or action-tokenization site is introduced. For calibration episode j, the inputs are the recorded observation history h_j, language instruction l_j, simulator state z_j, denoising seed xi_j, and simulator seed eta_j from an existing RoboTwin 2.0 or LIBERO episode, while F_0 is the full-precision WAM and F_r is the same model with only site r using the matched low-bit numerical quantizer and every other target site left at FP16. Running F_0 and F_r with the same history, seed, cache, and coupled future-video/action inference produces full action chunks a_j0 and a_jr; the first executable action from each chunk is then applied from the identical simulator state z_j to obtain next observations o_(j+1)0 and o_(j+1)r. Define d_a(j,r) as the mean squared difference between the two full action chunks divided by max(sigma_a^2, epsilon_a^2), where sigma_a^2 is the mean coordinate variance of FP16 full action chunks over the calibration episodes and epsilon_a is a named normalized-scale floor; define d_o(j,r) as the mean per-pixel absolute next-observation difference divided by max(sigma_o, epsilon_o), where sigma_o is the calibration mean per-pixel standard deviation. With lambda_a=lambda_o=1 as the default dimensionless weights, selected before held-out evaluation and sensitivity-checked over a named ratio sweep, C_r is the episode mean of lambda_a d_a plus lambda_o d_o, and its top-B sites replace the local PTQ ranking under the same high-precision budget B as the matched baseline. The C00 reframe supplies a computable responsibility-like object and the operator substitution makes it actionable for numerical weight/activation precision selection, while the C02 diagnostic compares C_r with the same-context local discrepancy L_r and uses the simulator's native contact or task-transition flag to report the divergence without treating the evaluated WAM as its own oracle. This closes the additive gap because a single-site perturbation is followed through the coupled action output and an externally simulated next observation, and it closes the subtractive gap because the same paired intervention tests whether L_r is sufficient rather than assuming it. The candidate remains a coupled future-prediction/action-generation WAM method: the minimal Fast-WAM path uses the official released checkpoint in idm mode with sigma_shift_FW=1.0 and the current infer_action path, not first_frame, and numerical W/A quantization only; an optional LingBot-VA replication must use the README's VAE/text-encoder CPU offload and budget backward calibration separately from its reported inference memory. Fake-quant or dequantized GEMM is used only for profiling and must be reported separately from final measurements with the real low-bit kernel.",
+  "core_mechanism_reasoning": "BLOCK 1 — PREMISES ledger\nP1 — Coupled closed-loop premise: each denoising call produces an action chunk and later calls consume observations affected by earlier actions; this is supported by the WAM formulation and the QuantWAMs pipeline record, but the size of a quantization-induced downstream effect is untested — falsification target.\nP2 — Single-site intervention premise: the public WAM/PTQ implementation can execute one named weight or activation site at the matched low-bit setting while the remaining target sites stay at FP16 and the same stochastic state is restored; this is an implementation bet — untested — falsification target.\nP3 — External transition premise: RoboTwin 2.0 and LIBERO provide existing simulator states, action execution, next observations, and native task/contact state needed to compare the two branches without inventing a new large dataset; this is believed true because both are the stated public robot-manipulation evaluation resources.\nP4 — Consequence relevance premise: a perturbation that changes the full action chunk and the next simulator observation is more deployment-relevant than a one-call local discrepancy; this follows directly from the Phase 1 bottleneck, but the ranking advantage is untested — falsification target.\nP5 — Contact-regime premise: local discrepancy and downstream consequence can rank sites differently around contact or task-state transitions; this is a domain bet motivated by contact-sensitive manipulation, and is untested — falsification target.\nobservation-model: calibration inputs are sampled as complete episodes from the available RoboTwin 2.0 and LIBERO splits, not as iid frames; histories are temporally dependent, task and embodiment proportions are inherited from the manifest, and simulator states are selected by the recorded trajectory, so C_r estimates the calibration episode distribution rather than an unbiased deployment distribution. Episode-level resampling and a held-out task/episode split are required.\nESTIMAND: for each site r, C_r estimates the mean dimensionless one-control-transition consequence of intervening on r over the calibration episode distribution D_cal, conditional on the same h_j, l_j, denoising seed xi_j, simulator state z_j, and simulator seed eta_j; it is not an unbiased estimate of global task-success loss or of an arbitrary real-robot deployment population.\nlineage constraint: the load-bearing artifact is the paired action-to-simulator-observation counterfactual, not n1 layer-local PTQ, n2 static sensitivity allocation, n3 QuantWM latent rollout/planning, or n4 QuantWAMs joint empirical-Fisher scoring and fixed-count schedule repair.\nBLOCK 2 — NAIVE-BASELINE AUDIT (branch (i))\nThe naive version is runnable-in-principle local PTQ: compute each site's FP16-versus-low-bit local output discrepancy on open-loop calibration inputs and upgrade the top-B sites. It does not already work because it silently assumes that local discrepancy is a sufficient proxy for downstream consequence, and that premise is false in this setting: a changed action chunk changes the next observation and therefore the inputs to later coupled predictions and contact dynamics. The mechanism confronts that failure head-on with paired simulator counterfactuals; ordinary local sensitivity or one-step PTQ does not resolve the WAM-specific combination of coupled video/action denoising, action-mediated state transition, and contact-critical feedback, so this is not a textbook tool application with the structure removed.\nBLOCK 3 — design rationale\nBoth gaps share one intervention registry and one C_r object: the anchor uses it to allocate precision, while the sibling uses the same contexts to isolate whether L_r agrees with it, so no second architecture or dataset is needed. A replay-only local profile in the style of QuantWAMs was rejected because it never executes the changed action to obtain the next observation, and a full Jacobian-product or latent-rollout planner was rejected because it would add unobserved assumptions and move the branch into the excluded latent planning route. The chosen design therefore preserves the WAM's coupled future/action path, adds only a one-site causal diagnostic and matched allocation operator, and explicitly avoids the occupied ancestral nodes and the already-covered QuantWAMs mechanisms.",
+  "core_mechanism_steps": "1. Freeze the public artifacts and inference contract. Use the exact official Fast-WAM released checkpoint selected for the run manifest as checkpoint_FW, set mode_FW=idm, sigma_shift_FW=1.0, and inference_path_FW=infer_action with the README's current no-default-VAE-decoding behavior; do not substitute first_frame because it removes the future branch. Set b_lo to the matched QuantWAMs W4A4-dominant numerical weight/activation setting, keep the action/video coupling unchanged, and output a versioned configuration manifest with the checkpoint filename, repository revision, mode, sigma shift, inference path, and low-bit kernels. The minimal validation uses Fast-WAM only; if LingBot-VA is added, use its official VAE/text-encoder CPU-offload inference configuration and report backward-calibration memory as a separate measurement rather than reusing the reported inference-memory figure.\n2. Build an episode-level calibration manifest from existing RoboTwin 2.0 and LIBERO simulator episodes. Set N_cal=32 as the default to match the fixed-trajectory scale reported for QuantWAMs, with a pre-registered 16/32/64 episode sensitivity check; preserve observation histories, instructions, simulator states, action chunks, denoising seeds xi_j, simulator seeds eta_j, and native contact/task-transition flags. Output D_cal and a held-out episode/task split without mixing adjacent frames across splits.\n3. Enumerate the intervention registry R from the public WAM implementation. Each r=(branch, module, denoising_step, axis) identifies one actual quantization hook in the coupled video/action denoiser, with axis in {weight, activation}; exclude VAE-only, text-only, latent/action tokenizer, and planner-only sites. Output a deterministic registry with a lexicographic tie-break order and a mapping from each site to its kernel and bit setting.\n4. Measure single-site model interventions with matched stochastic state. For every r and calibration item j, restore the immutable FP16 model and persistent cache, run F_0 and F_r with the same h_j, l_j, xi_j, and inference path, and record the full action chunks a_j0 and a_jr plus the local output discrepancy L_r. Use fake-quant/dequantized GEMM only as a profiling implementation; record whether it was used and keep it separate from the real low-bit-kernel evaluation. Output paired model traces indexed by (j,r).\n5. Execute paired environment counterfactuals. Restore the same simulator state z_j and eta_j for the two traces, apply the first executable action from each full chunk, and record o_(j+1)0, o_(j+1)r, and the simulator's independent contact/task-transition state. The downstream output of this step is the actual next observation and state transition caused by the altered action, not merely a changed intermediate discrepancy.\n6. Compute the consequence object. Calculate sigma_a from the coordinate variance of FP16 full action chunks and sigma_o from the mean per-pixel standard deviation of FP16 next observations; use named floors epsilon_a=epsilon_o=1e-3 in normalized units, with a pre-registered 1e-4/1e-3/1e-2 sensitivity check. Compute d_a and d_o as the normalized full-action-chunk and next-observation differences, set lambda_a=lambda_o=1 by default with a held-out sensitivity sweep over lambda_a/lambda_o in {0.5,1,2}, and output C_r as their episode mean with bootstrap intervals over complete episodes.\n7. Replace the local allocation operator without retraining. Set B to the exact high-precision-site count or matched bit-cost of the QuantWAMs comparison schedule, rank sites by C_r, and upgrade the top-B sites while leaving all other sites at b_lo; resolve ties by the registry order. This modifies the numerical precision assignment and therefore the downstream generated action chunk, while preserving the same checkpoint, denoising schedule, data, and model architecture.\n8. Run the confound-isolating diagnostic. On the same episodes, compare L_r and C_r using Spearman rank correlation separately on simulator-identified contact/task-transition and non-contact subsets, then compare top-B C_r allocation against top-B L_r allocation and a score-permuted negative control. The reported object is the divergence in downstream action/observation and task metrics, not a claim that a correlation itself is causal.\n9. Validate the selected schedule on held-out RoboTwin 2.0 and LIBERO episodes with the real low-bit kernel. Compare FP16, the matched local-fidelity baseline, the C_r allocation, and uniform precision at the same B; measure task success, contact-sensitive failure rate, full action-chunk deviation, next-observation deviation, peak memory, and latency, with the same Fast-WAM configuration and multiple episode seeds.\n10. Keep CPU arithmetic checks and deployment claims separate. CPU runs may verify site indexing, deterministic paired seeds, scale normalization, and C_r arithmetic only; they do not establish robot success, GPU memory, or speedup. Report any LingBot-VA replication as a separate calibration/inference experiment with its offload and checkpoint configuration, and do not combine fake-quant traces with real-kernel performance.",
+  "falsification_prediction": "Run the minimal experiment on the exact Fast-WAM checkpoint_FW in idm mode with sigma_shift_FW=1.0 and infer_action, using a small held-out RoboTwin 2.0/LIBERO manipulation split, the same low-bit setting b_lo, and the same high-precision budget B for local-score and C_r allocations. If the candidate works, the contact-transition subset will show a larger local-score-versus-C_r ranking divergence and the C_r schedule will reduce held-out contact-sensitive task failures and next-observation deviation relative to the matched local-score schedule, without changing the architecture or precision budget. The single load-bearing variable is C_r, the paired downstream consequence index; permute C_r across the site registry before selecting top-B sites, and the predicted effect is that the downstream contact-sensitive success/failure advantage disappears even though the quantizer and evaluation remain unchanged. A positive control is the oracle simulator-state branch that exposes the independent transition outcome during calibration; it should preserve more of the C_r allocation advantage than a model-only local discrepancy if the mechanism, rather than calibration noise, is carrying the result.",
+  "compute_budget": "1.2 GPU-day on an 80GB-class equivalent for a staged Fast-WAM-only validation (approximately two short single-GPU A100-40GB stages, no large-model training) + $0 paid API; LingBot-VA replication, if later added, requires a separate calibration-memory and inference budget.",
+  "differentiation_from_lit": [
+    {
+      "paper_id": "arxiv:2607.28405v1",
+      "delta": "QuantWAMs scores local block and denoising-step discrepancies on replayed full-precision states and repairs a fixed-count protection schedule, whereas this candidate constructs a paired single-site counterfactual that executes the altered action from the same simulator state and uses the resulting next observation as the precision-selection signal."
+    },
+    {
+      "paper_id": "arxiv:2602.02110v1",
+      "delta": "QuantWM studies weight/activation PTQ and long-horizon error in a DINO-WM latent planning setting, whereas this candidate measures the action-chunk and next-observation consequence of numerical perturbations inside a coupled video/action manipulation WAM."
+    },
+    {
+      "paper_id": "semanticscholar:b5be12ccbb037be57a1dbc03fbd43d379170055f",
+      "delta": "Fast-WAM isolates video co-training from explicit future imagination and does not provide a numerical PTQ consequence signal, whereas this candidate keeps the future/action-coupled idm feasibility path and adds single-site weight/activation intervention with consequence-based allocation."
+    }
+  ],
+  "almost_prior_paper_id": "arxiv:2607.28405v1",
+  "what_step_was_missed": "The closest paper would have needed to construct and execute a paired simulator counterfactual in which a single module or denoising-step perturbation changes the action chunk from a fixed state, then measure the resulting next observation or task transition and use that marginal consequence for precision selection; its replayed local discrepancy does not construct this action-mediated transition artifact.",
+  "signature_terms": [
+    "single-site closed-loop consequence score",
+    "action and next-observation counterfactuals",
+    "contact-critical mixed-precision selection",
+    "coupled video-action denoising"
+  ],
+  "alias_terms": [
+    "task-loss-aware quantization calibration",
+    "model-error-aware robust MPC",
+    "teacher-student behavior matching",
+    "off-policy marginal effect estimation",
+    "interventional policy sensitivity analysis"
+  ],
+  "composition_note": ""
+}
